a. provision of facilities and infrastructure for Child health;  
b. provision of human resources in the health sector;  
c. guidance on Protection and maintenance of Child health, covering:  
1\. guidance on child growth health in efforts to reduce infant mortality rates and malnutrition rates for children,  
2\. guidance on providing early breastfeeding initiation, exclusive breast milk and continuous breast milk directly,  
3\. guidance on providing complete immunization for children, and  
4\. guidance on providing healthy, balanced, and safe food for Children.  
d. provision of facilities and infrastructure for Child welfare, covering:  
1\. guidance on child-friendly households,  
2\. provision of child-friendly public spaces,  
3\. guidance on availability of child-friendly infrastructure in public spaces,  
4\.guidance on organizing Children's playgrounds,  
5\.guidance on Child care facilities,  
6\.guidance on availability of breastfeeding (lactation) rooms;  
7\. guidance on availability of child-friendly places of worship,  
8\. provision and guidance on availability of green open spaces,  
9\. guidance on organizing smoke-free areas, and  
10\. guidance on organizing child-friendly public transportation,  
e. provision of facilities and infrastructure for accessibility for Children with disabilities,  
f. maintenance, care, and social rehabilitation of neglected children,  
g. guidance on improving the welfare of poor families.  
2. Guidance on child-friendly households as referred to in paragraph (1) letter d number 1, is carried out in an effort to fulfill access to clean water and proper sanitation.

#### Article 21

Fulfillment of the right to education, leisure time, culture, and recreation as referred to in Article 14 letter d, is implemented through:  
a. provision of educational facilities and infrastructure;  
b. provision of educational human resources;  
c. organizing child-friendly schools;  
d. implementation of support for the 12 (twelve) year compulsory education program;  
e. guidance on early childhood education;  
f. facilitation of access to education for children who are already married;  
g. facilitation of access to education for children in conflict with the law;  
h. provision of facilities and infrastructure for children's playgrounds;  
i. provision of creative and recreational facilities and infrastructure, and  
j. provision of sports facilities and infrastructure for Children with disabilities.

# CHAPTER V

## SPECIAL PROTECTION OF CHILDREN

## Part One

General

#### Article 22

1. Special Protection as referred to in Article 4 letter d, includes:  
a. Children in emergency situations;  
b. Child victims of child marriage;  
c. Children in conflict with the law;  
d. Children from minority and isolated groups;  
e. Children who are exploited economically and/or sexually;  
f. Children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic, and other addictive substance abuse;  
g. Children who are victims of pornography;  
h. Children with Human Immunodeficiency Virus and Acquired Immune Deficiency Syndrome;  
i. Child victims of kidnapping, sale, and/or trafficking;  
j. Child victims of physical and/or psychological Violence;  
k. Child victims of sexual crimes;  
l. Child victims of terrorism networks;  
m. Children with Disabilities;  
n. Child victims of maltreatment and neglect;  
o. Children with deviant social behavior, and  
p. Children who are victims of stigmatization from labeling related to their parents' condition.  
2. Children in emergency situations as referred to in paragraph (1) letter a, consist of:  
a. Children who become refugees;  
b. Child victims of riots;  
c. Child victims of natural disasters and non-natural disasters, and  
d. Children in armed conflict situations.  
3. Child victims of child marriage as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b, constitutes a marriage conducted between a man and a woman where one and/or both are still of child age.

#### Article 23

The implementation of Special Protection of Children as referred to in Article 22 paragraph (1), includes:  
a. Prevention, and  
b. Handling.

## Part Two

Prevention

#### Article 24

Prevention as referred to in Article 23 letter a, includes:  
a. optimization of child resource capacity;  
b. creation of an environment that supports child Protection;  
c. improvement of accessibility and expansion of services;  
d. strengthening of regulations and institutions, and  
e. strengthening of stakeholder coordination.

#### Article 25

1. Optimization of child resource capacity as referred to in Article 4 letter a is carried out through:  
a. increasing awareness and attitudes of children regarding the right to receive Protection;  
b. increasing child participation in child Protection efforts; and  
c. increasing spaces and communities that support children to grow and develop optimally, both in formal and informal environments in line with the development of information, technology, and children's situations.  
2. Increasing awareness and attitudes of children regarding the right to receive Protection as referred to in paragraph (1) letter a is carried out through local government policy intervention towards optimization of child Protection implementation in school education.  
3. Increasing child participation in child Protection efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b, is carried out through regional policy intervention:  
a. providing and strengthening peer counselors and Youth Posyandu starting from the village/kelurahan level to the district level integrated with the formal referral system,  
b. providing formal participation mechanisms for children and adolescents to be directly involved in child Protection efforts at various levels, one of which is through the Children's Forum, and  
c. strengthening the Children's Forum as pioneers and reporters.

#### Article 26

Creation of an environment that supports child Protection as referred to in Article 24 letter b, is carried out through:  
a. change of values, norms, and perspectives on child Protection;  
b. strengthening the role of parents, schools, families, and communities in child Protection, and.  
c. revitalization of cultures that risk encouraging violence and child marriage.  
2. Change of values, norms, and perspectives on child Protection as referred to in paragraph (1) letter a is carried out through local government policy intervention strategies towards:  
a. strengthening understanding and the role of parents, families, social/community organizations, schools, Islamic boarding schools and other stakeholders in child Protection efforts;  
b. transformation of counseling and assistance services for parents and children professionally;  
c. improvement of quality parenting skills especially for adolescents, and  
d. encouraging cultural practices that support the realization of child Protection and prevent child marriage.  
3. Strengthening the role of parents, schools, families, communities and other stakeholders in child Protection as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b is carried out through local government policy intervention strategies towards:  
a. socialization and education about the importance of child Protection;  
b. family economic empowerment through family entrepreneurship, family hope programs and/or other programs that ensure children from poor and vulnerable families receive social assistance;  
c. strengthening the system and environment of child-friendly schools by adding reproductive health Rights and mental health, and  
d. strengthening community institutions at various levels up to the village/kelurahan level with various training and child assistance skills.

